nerally has to be released under GPL-3.0. That means offering the source to your recipients, which conflicts with shipping closed source.\n- Distribution is what triggers this. SaaS-only use, where users never receive the code, usually doesn't. GPL-3.0 has no network clause, unlike AGPL. If you ship binaries, an installer, a container image, or a desktop or mobile app, it applies.\n- GPL-3.0 also has anti-tivoization and patent-termination terms. These make it harder to live with in embedded or locked-down devices.\n\nYour options:\n1. **Replace it** with a permissively licensed alternative. This is usually the cleanest fix.\n2. **Ask the author for a commercial license.** Many GPL projects offer one, but only if the author holds all the copyrights.\n3. **Isolate it** as a separate process that talks over a CLI or socket. This is legally gray. Have counsel review it before you rely on it.\n4. **Open-source your product under GPL-3.0**, if that's acceptable to you.\n\n**The other two dependencies are fine for closed source**\n- **requests (Apache-2.0)**: Keep the license text and NOTICE file. If you modified the library, say so. It includes a patent grant.\n- **left-pad (MIT)**: Include the copyright notice and license text in your attribution.\n\n**Limits of this check**\n- `deps.txt` only lists direct dependencies. Transitive dependencies may carry their own licenses, so run a license scanner over the full tree.\n- The license labels in the file are self-reported. I haven't checked them against the actual packages. Confirm that `somelib` really is GPL-3.0, and whether it's GPL-only or dual-licensed.\n- Some libraries add exceptions, such as LGPL-style linking exceptions, that change the analysis.\n- I'm not a lawyer. Since you're about to ship, have legal counsel confirm the somelib decision.\n\nBefore release, also add a third-party notices file that covers the Apache and MIT attributions.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}